A sentinel error is an error value created once and exported so callers can test for it. The shape is always the same:

```go
// package store
var ErrNotFound = errors.New("record not found")

func Get(id string) (*Item, error) {
    // ...
    return nil, ErrNotFound   // the same value, every time
}
```

And the caller side:

```go
item, err := store.Get(id)
if errors.Is(err, store.ErrNotFound) {
    // distinguishable from other failures
}
```

The naming convention is deliberate: Err-prefixed, so it reads as a condition at the use site. Because the package creates the value once and returns that same value on every failure, callers can reliably identify the case — and because errors.Is is used rather than ==, the match still works if an intermediate layer wrapped the error with %w while adding context.

Sentinels suit a small, stable set of conditions callers branch on: not-found, already-exists, permission-denied. When the set grows or the errors need to carry data (which record? what status?), custom error types take over — but the sentinel is the right first tool and the standard library is full of them (io.EOF, context.DeadlineExceeded, sql.ErrNoRows).

errors.New gives callers exactly one bit of information: which sentinel it is. If a caller needs to know more — which key wasn't found, what the retry-after is, whether the failure is retryable — a bare string error can't say. The caller can't parse the message, because messages are for humans and change.

The progression looks like this:

```go
// 1. sentinel — matchable, no data
var ErrRateLimited = errors.New("rate limited")

// 2. typed — matchable AND data-carrying
type RateLimitError struct {
    RetryAfter time.Duration
}
func (e *RateLimitError) Error() string {
    return fmt.Sprintf("rate limited, retry after %v", e.RetryAfter)
}

// caller
type target *RateLimitError
if errors.As(err, &target) {
    time.Sleep(target.RetryAfter)  // the data the caller needed
    retry()
}
```

errors.As finds the type anywhere in the wrap chain and gives the caller the struct — with fields, not prose. The sentinel answered 'was it rate limiting?'; the type also answers 'for how long?'.

So the honest summary: errors.New is perfect when the identity is the whole story. The moment the caller's next action depends on data about the failure — and retry-backoff, redirect targets, and which-record questions all do — the error needs to be a type. Not a string that the caller regexes; a value with fields.
